    "question": {
      ":question/id": "184d74a1-d20a-579b-93ce-d8754e008576",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j+{z}^{2}\\,k$\n- **II.** $S(x,y,z)={e}^{-2z}\\sqrt{x+y}$\n- **III.** $Z(t)=3t\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j-\\sqrt{t}\\,k$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point $(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}$ to a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $S(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{3}$ to values in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $Z(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
