    "question": {
      ":question/id": "42f6e5a9-a222-547f-a4d5-591afd32cca6",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{3}?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $D(x,y)={x}^{4}\\,i-{y}^{4}\\,j$\n- **II.** $J(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-y\\,j+z\\,k$\n- **III.** $U(t)={e}^{t}\\,i-t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{3}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{3}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $D(x,y)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}$ (not ${R}^{3}$).\n\n- The function $J(x,y,z)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS=\\{(x,y,z)\\in {R}^{3}\\,|\\,x\\ne n\\pi ,\\,n\\in Z\\}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $U(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$ It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{3}$) to vectors in ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
